Record the phase: `work state set --phase=leg1-prep`

Leg 1 produces exactly one thing: a version-bump + changelog-roll commit on
a branch off `prep-release`, delivered through an MR targeting
`prep-release`, with the bumped version and the bump-MR merge SHA recorded
in run state. Nothing in this leg touches `main`, tags, or GitHub.

Before starting, run `work release status`. A recorded release version AND
bump-MR merge SHA mean leg 1 already completed — skip to the gate. A
recorded version with no bump SHA means the bump commit was made and
recorded — check how far it got (remote `prep-<X.Y.Z>` branch? open bump
MR?) and pick up at the first missing step (6, 7, or 8), adopting whatever
already exists per the adoption rule in step 6.

### 1. Branch off `prep-release`

```
git fetch origin prep-release
git switch -c release-bump origin/prep-release
```

Always branch off up-to-date `origin/prep-release` — never off `main`, and
never commit to `prep-release` directly. The branch is renamed to its final
`prep-<X.Y.Z>` name in step 4, once the dry run has confirmed the version.

### 2. Install ctl at the pinned ref

Until ctl itself ships through this flow, it installs from its canonical
git.20c.com repository at a PINNED commit — never a branch, never HEAD.

The container carries no persistent credential helper for `git.20c.com`, and
`~/.gitconfig` is a read-only mount. A bare `uv tool install` from that host
therefore fails with `could not read Username for 'https://git.20c.com':
terminal prompts disabled`. The generic `GITLAB_TOKEN` is scoped to its
issuing host and must never be sent elsewhere. `LMER_GITLAB_TOKEN_HOST` names
that host explicitly; when it is unset, the host is derived from
`LMER_WORK_REPO`, matching lmer's token lookup contract. The command below
enforces that the resolved host is `git.20c.com` before the token is expanded.
A missing token, an unknown issuing host, or a different issuing host is a hard
stop naming the credential requirement.

Supply that token through lmer's action-free ASKPASS helper. The helper only
answers Git's username and password prompts from the environment passed to the
one install process; it performs no network or filesystem action, and the
credential never enters a URL, argv, Git config, or persistent file:

```
# Pinned ctl commit. Bumping this pin is a reviewed edit to this taskdef,
# never a session-time decision; the dry run below revalidates it.
# Pin provenance: the `feature/release-flow-fixes` merge (MR !11) on the
# canonical repo's main, verified against that repo directly — both the
# path and the commit were resolved with credentials, not inferred from
# the GitHub mirror. That MR is what makes this leg work at all: it fixed
# the case-insensitive residual changelog key and added the version write
# with no git side effects that step 4 uses.
# NOTE ON THE PATH: the canonical ctl lives under the `gh/` namespace.
# There is a same-named `20c/ctl` project on the instance which is a STALE
# copy (no `main` branch) and does NOT contain this commit — pointing the
# install there fails with "upload-pack: not our ref".
CTL_PINNED_REF=5af16ceaa103095f53594fe6aa33246d05f72ba7

GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${LMER_GITLAB_TOKEN_HOST:-}"
if [ -z "$GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST" ]; then
  GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${LMER_WORK_REPO:-}"
  case "$GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST" in
    *://*)
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST#*://}"
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST%%/*}"
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST##*@}"
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST%%:*}"
      ;;
    *@*:*)
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST##*@}"
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="${GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST%%:*}"
      ;;
    *)
      GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST=""
      ;;
  esac
fi

GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST="$(printf '%s' "$GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST" | sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//;s/[[:space:]]*$//' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
if [ "$GITLAB_TOKEN_ISSUING_HOST" != "git.20c.com" ]; then
  printf '%s\n' "ctl install requires GITLAB_TOKEN issued by git.20c.com; set LMER_GITLAB_TOKEN_HOST or LMER_WORK_REPO so the issuing host can be verified" >&2
  exit 1
fi

LC_ALL=C \
  GIT_ASKPASS=/Agents/global/bin/lmer-git-askpass \
  GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 \
  LMER_GIT_ASKPASS_USERNAME=oauth2 \
  LMER_GIT_ASKPASS_PASSWORD="${GITLAB_TOKEN}" \
  uv tool install "git+https://git.20c.com/gh/20c/ctl@${CTL_PINNED_REF}"
```

ctl needs no `Ctl/config.yml` in the target repository — the operations
below dispatch by plugin type. `ctl --help` listing no configured
operations is expected and is NOT a sign the install is broken.

If the install fails, that is a hard stop: record it with `work state set
--stop-reason=critical_error --critical-error '{"summary": "ctl install
failed at pinned ref", "detail": "<ref and error>"}'`, `work commit`, and
end the session. Do NOT fall back to PyPI, a floating branch, a different
ref, or a hand-written bump script. The pin is human-owned: report the
failure and leave moving it to a reviewed taskdef edit.

### 3. Dry-run before committing anything

The changelog mechanism is the resolved `release.changelog` parameter from
Phase 0.5 — exactly one of:

- `legacy` — ctl rolls `CHANGELOG.yaml`: the `unreleased:` section becomes
  the new `X.Y.Z` section.
- `fragments` — ctl compiles the `changelog.d/` fragment files into the
  changelog and removes the consumed fragments (lmer's first release under
  this flow runs in this mode: the pinned ctl carries fragment support, and
  lmer already accumulates its unreleased entries as fragments, so a legacy
  roll would silently leave them behind).

Run the version bump + changelog roll in dry-run mode FIRST — no writes, no
commits to the real tree.

ctl has NO dry-run flag at this pin: every operation below writes in place.
The dry run is therefore step 4's commands run for real against a THROWAWAY
CLONE, never against the working checkout:

```
DRYRUN="$(mktemp -d)/dryrun"
git clone --quiet --no-hardlinks --branch release-bump . "$DRYRUN"
cd "$DRYRUN"
```

Run step 4's commands there, then read `git status --short` and
`git diff`. Discard the directory afterwards — nothing from it is ever
copied back into the working checkout.

The dry run must show all three:

1. the target version `X.Y.Z` in `pyproject.toml`,
2. a changelog roll in the configured mechanism — the `CHANGELOG.yaml`
   section roll for `legacy`, fragment compilation for `fragments`, and
3. the diff shape step 4 requires, with nothing outside it.

Read the changelog result by PARSING it, not by reading the diff: the roll
rewrites the whole file (see step 4), so a wrong or partial roll does not
stand out visually. Confirm the new `X.Y.Z` section carries the entries you
expect, that the residual section is reset to empty lists, and that the
historical sections are semantically unchanged.

A dry run that fails, or that handles the changelog in any mechanism other
than the configured one, means the pinned ref does not handle this
repository: HARD STOP with the same critical-error recording contract,
naming the pinned ref and the configured changelog mode. Do NOT bump the
pin yourself and do NOT hand-edit the changelog to compensate — a human
revalidates and moves the pin.

Idempotency — check this BEFORE reading a failure as a pin problem. When a
previous release run died after its bump MR merged, `prep-release` already
carries the bump, and the dry run fails on the ALREADY-DONE work rather
than on the pin: `ctl changelog release` reports the release version
already exists in the changelog, and `pyproject.toml` on `prep-release`
already reads `X.Y.Z`. That is a resumable state, NOT a hard stop. Do NOT
bump again: record the version read from `pyproject.toml` on `prep-release`
and the merge SHA of the bump MR that landed it (step 8's recording
contract), then proceed to the gate — see "Resuming an in-progress
release".

The hard stop above is for a dry run that fails with the version NOT yet
bumped on `prep-release`, or that handles the changelog in the wrong
mechanism. Distinguish the two by reading the version on `prep-release`
before concluding anything.

### 4. Run the bump + changelog roll

Three commands, in this order, from the repository root:

```
ctl version set <X.Y.Z> .        # writes the version field in pyproject.toml
ctl changelog release <X.Y.Z>    # rolls the changelog, consumes fragments
uv lock                          # refreshes the project's version in uv.lock
```

- `ctl version set` performs NO git operations at all — no pull, no commit,
  no tag, no push. That is why it is the operation used here: the commit is
  `gate-commit`'s job and the tag is leg 2's.
- The trailing `.` is required. Without a repository argument ctl has no
  target and fails.
- Do NOT pass `--init`. It creates an untracked `Ctl/VERSION` file, which
  is an unexpected entry in the diff below. A repository whose version
  lives only in `pyproject.toml` is a valid target without it.
- `uv lock` runs last because it reads the version `ctl version set` wrote.

FORBIDDEN — these commit, tag and push from inside ctl, bypassing the
commit gate and creating an unsigned tag that is leg 2's job under a name
leg 2 does not use:

- `ctl version bump <segment>` without `--no-git`
- `ctl version tag <X.Y.Z>`

When the run was given a bump SEGMENT rather than an exact version, use
`ctl version bump <segment> --no-git .` in place of `ctl version set`. It
validates the changelog first, so it must run AFTER `ctl changelog release`
— `ctl version set` has no such ordering constraint, which is why it is the
default form here.

Then verify the working tree. The diff must contain exactly:

- `pyproject.toml` — the version field, one line
- `uv.lock` — the project's OWN `version` line, one line. This entry is
  expected and does not violate the "nothing else" rule; a `uv.lock` diff
  that touches anything beyond the project's own version line means
  dependencies moved and IS a stop-and-ask.
- `CHANGELOG.yaml` — the roll
- `changelog.d/*.yaml` — the consumed fragments, deleted (fragments mode)
- `CHANGELOG.md` — regenerated by the roll. `git add` it; on a repository
  releasing through this flow for the first time it is untracked and would
  otherwise be left out of the commit.

Anything outside that set is a stop-and-ask, not something to commit
around.

KNOWN AND EXPECTED — do not stop for these:

- The roll rewrites the WHOLE `CHANGELOG.yaml`, not just the new section:
  list indentation, long-string wrapping and non-ASCII escaping all change,
  so the diff is far larger than the entries added. Confirm the content by
  parsing (step 3), not by reading the diff.
- The rewrite DROPS every comment in `CHANGELOG.yaml` — YAML round-trip
  does not preserve them. Known ctl limitation, accepted for now, tracked
  as ctl issue #32; do not hand-restore them in this commit.

Rename the branch to its final name: `git branch -m prep-<X.Y.Z>`.

### 5. Commit, then record the version — BEFORE anything is pushed

- Stage every path from step 4's expected diff — including the deleted
  fragments and the regenerated `CHANGELOG.md`, which is untracked on a
  first release and would otherwise be silently left out.
- Commit through `gate-commit` — the standard commit gate; never raw
  `git commit`. Commit message: `prep <X.Y.Z>`.
- Record leg-1 state immediately — run state fields come from the
  runstate subsystem; read and write them through the `work` CLI only,
  never edit state files by hand and never invent a parallel record of
  your own:
  - `work release record version <X.Y.Z>` — the `pyproject.toml` version,
    write-once, NO `v` prefix (the tag prefix is applied in leg 2). This
    is also where the RUN takes its own address: recording the version
    moves the run dir to `release-<repo>-v<X.Y.Z>` and frees the seed
    address for the next release (the verb prints the new path). Cite that
    path — not the seed one — in anything you write from here on; a URL to
    the old path 404s. Nothing else changes: the run resolves by content,
    so `work` verbs and a relaunch still find it.
  - `work log` the bump branch name, so a relaunch finds the branch
    without archaeology.
  - `work commit` so the recorded state pushes now, not at session end.

The ordering is load-bearing: the version is recorded before the branch
leaves this machine, so a session dying mid-push resumes at
`leg1-record-bump-merge` and adopts what exists — it never rolls a second
bump against a branch that is already on the remote.

### 6. Push the branch

Adoption, never re-creation: check `git ls-remote origin
refs/heads/prep-<X.Y.Z>` (and for an open bump MR titled `prep <X.Y.Z>`)
FIRST. An existing remote branch or open bump MR matching the dry-run's
target version means a previous session died between its push and its
record landing — ADOPT it: fetch it, verify it carries exactly the bump
(the version bump + changelog roll of step 4, nothing else), record the
version if not yet recorded (step 5's recording contract), and pick up at
step 7 or 8 as appropriate. NEVER re-create the branch and NEVER
force-push over it.

Otherwise push: `git push -u origin prep-<X.Y.Z>`. Never push
`prep-release` or `main` directly.

### 7. Open the bump MR targeting `prep-release` — then log its URL

Open the MR from `prep-<X.Y.Z>` targeting `prep-release` — NEVER targeting
`main`. The MR into `main` already exists: the standing `prep-release` →
`main` MR, which becomes the release MR once this bump lands. Title the
bump MR `prep <X.Y.Z>` and note the release run in its description.

Then `work log` the bump-MR URL, so a relaunch finds the MR without
archaeology, and `work commit`.

### 8. The bump MR lands — record the merge SHA

Once the bump MR is merged into `prep-release` (its pipeline green):

- `work release record bump-sha <sha>` — the full 40-hex merge SHA on
  `prep-release`.
- `work commit`.

With the version and bump SHA both recorded, leg 1 is complete and the
standing `prep-release` → `main` MR IS the release MR — proceed to the
gate. A relaunch decides whether the bump landed from these two recorded
fields alone, never from an earlier session's memory.
